A||N||12||FieroFanaticus2||Simple question about adding a rear wing||||5||
Z||000000||FieroFanaticus2||05-17-2001||10:28 AM||||Just a quick question this time.<BR>I am adding a spoiler this weekend and want to know if there is any certain type of drill bit that I should use to drill through the rear deck lid?  I don't need any cracks or giant paint chips.  Thanks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroJoe||05-17-2001||10:31 AM||||Buy a brand new bit, so it will be sharp.  A regular wood/metal twist drill bit should work fine.  No need for Forstner or boring bits or anything like that.<P>Also, put a piece of tape over the area you are going to drill, and then drill right through the tape.  I do this sometimes with wood that would splinter easily and it helps keep the splinters down.<P>Go slow with a sharp bit, and then you should be fine.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroFanaticus2||05-17-2001||10:34 AM||||wow, that was quick!  Thanks.  Another quickie though -- should I maybe use a smaller bit first and then go to the actual sized bit?  Or would that just be a waste of time?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||05-17-2001||10:50 AM||||With soft material like fiberglass, or the stuff the deck lid is made of, you'll probably get more tearout with a pilot hole,and you aren't drilling a hole that big anyway. I'd skip the smaller bit and just use the bit for the final size. You can use a nail or a center punch to locate the hole, just don't hit it too hard, just a tap will do. Also, I disagree about going slow. Start off slow to get the hole position accurate, and then let 'er rip. When the bit starts to go through the other side, it'll grab, and actually tear the material rather than cutting it if you're going too slow. Just don't apply too much pressure, and apply tape to the bottom side as well as the top, since the bottom (or exit side of the drilled hole) will tend to splinter more than the top. It sounds worse than it is. Just think of it as drilling a hole.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroFanaticus2||05-17-2001||11:32 AM||||Ok, I figured it probably wasn't that difficult, I just didn't want to think that I actually knew what I was doing only to find out I didn't.  Thanks for the help!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||05-17-2001||11:55 AM||||Don't forget, you'll need the decklid springs from a car that had the wing. The wing adds weight and the spring tension is different on the winged cars.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">   <BR>Got Emblems?||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||05-17-2001||12:16 PM||||One other thing, if you have a GT (fastback), FOR GOD'S SAKE, DON'T DO IT!!!!!!! <P>Yours truly, <BR>The Smooth Deck Preservation Society||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroFanaticus2||05-17-2001||12:18 PM||||Naw, it's an '88 coupe.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||lawrence||05-17-2001||12:40 PM||||When you install the wing, put some huge flat washers behind the nuts.  This will make it harder for vandals to yank it.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/sts" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/sts</A>  - Sequential Turn Signals<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/products" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/products</A>  - Webelectric Products||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroFanaticus2||05-17-2001||01:20 PM||||Yeah, I went to the hardware store last night and picked up some giant flat washers, a set of locking washers, and a set of locking nuts.  I live in 'pretty' decent area, but I'm weary of those who travel for their 5 finger discounts.  I'll let ya'll know how it turns out if you're interested.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||05-17-2001||07:02 PM||||Big washers are a good idea except...there isn't any room for washers on the front two bolts.  The (what's the word for it?) strengthening bar, thick thing, whatever it's called (must be a simple word since I can't remember it) is in the way.  I used the stock washer/nuts (gotta be a word for those, too) for the front and big washers for the back bolts.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000011||StuGood||05-17-2001||07:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by stimpy:<BR><B>...The Smooth Deck Preservation Society...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks for the reminder - I need to renew my membership  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000012||fierohoho||05-17-2001||08:09 PM||||Oreif, can't you just adjust the setting on the torsion bar that supports the trunk lid. I put a wing on my '84 and set the torsion bar by relieving presure on the bar and moving the retaining pin to the rear most hole. Or is the '88 setup different than a '84?||||reg||1
